Reprinted without permission from The Boston Globe.
For the personal use only of members of the Extropians mailing list.


			  Cough Up  Counselor

			  by Robert L. Turner

You Social Security recipients, gasoline users and mortgage interest
deducters - everyone trembling at the threat of being drafted as cannon
fodder in Bill Clinton's war on the deficit: Rest easy.

Here's the answer, nice and simple.

Clinton sends out a letter to every lawyer in America stating that he or
she is being considered for appointment as attorney general.

The amount of money in unpaid taxes, withholding, Social Security and
penalties that would flood Washington in the following two weeks would
probably allow Clinton to wipe out the deficit, pay for national health
insurance and liberate Bosnia overnight.

You've heard of the Zoe Baird problem.  This is the Zoe Baird solution.
It's beautiful.

No one objects.  Only lawyers get burned.  It'll be just one more lawyer
joke, and everyone will laugh - even most of the lawyers, who don't like
to lose clients by being grumpy.

Actually, if Clinton gets help from The Reader's Digest people in
drafting his letter, each of the lawyers will believe he or she is a
possible winner.  The letters win be framed just as soon as the cheeks
are written.

Some of these situations involve big money.  Last week, just days after
Baird went back on its payroll, Aetna announced it wanted to stop
writing auto insurance in California.  Maybe the insurance colossus
decided it couldn't handle the California losses and Baird's
half-million, too.

As for the federal balance sheet, the news is already encouraging.

Baird paid $10,900 in Social Security taxes, interest and fines.

Kimba Wood's tax payments were apparently current.  But Charles Ruff,
another prospective attorney general, volunteered $3,300 before he was
scratched from the list.

Equally encouraging, the flap has already had an impact beyond this one
job.

Commerce Secretary Ron Brown paid $1,200 in back Social Security taxes
and interest.

A couple of members of Congress have fessed up.  Sen. Bob Graham of
Florida promised to cough up $75.

In New Jersey, two Republicans preparing to run against Gov. Jim Florio
have hit big numbers.

W. Cary Edwards, a former state attorney general, paid $2,820 despite
saying he had been misled by his Portuguese housekeeper.

And Christine Todd Whitman paid $14,467 to the Internal Revenue Service
and $2,205 to the Immigration and Naturalization Service.  Add to these
$8,227 she paid to the state and another amount she expects to send
Washington when interest and penalties are determined.

So we're talking serious money.

The nation boasts 777,119 lawyers, according to the American Bar
Association, though it often seems like 10 times that number.

Sure, some are childless and have no child-care expenses, but darned few
are homeless.  A number are married to lawyers, reducing the number of
lawyer households.  And there may even be some who have paid their
domestic help.

Let's say there are half a million live cases.  Double that to include
the ripple effect among non-AG candidates.  Multiply by $5,000 - a
conservative estimate of the average payment.  Now double that, at
least, to account for the income taxes to be collected from the workers.

Bingo - $10 billion.  All right, the deficit hasn't disappeared.  We'll
have to leave Bosnia to another day.  But this is big dough.

Bill Litant, spokesman for the Massachusetts Bar Association, who
supplied the figure for the number of lawyers, had a suggestion
yesterday for augmenting the take.  "How about including the press?" he
said.

This idea is ridiculous on its face.  Everyone knows what pathetic
chattel reporters are.  Domestic help is out of the question, except for
those of us who happen to be married to federal judges and such.  The
proceeds would be meager.  Besides, reporters can't add well enough to
fill out the forms.  More to the point, who would want to appoint us to
anything?

On the other hand, including the press would have one benefit: It could
make the scheme even more popular.


Robert L. Turner is a Globe columnist


